JSP-000078 / Erdos 58 — arbitrary-graph formalization and verification package

This bundle does not claim a new solution of Gyarfas's finite graph theorem,
or first formalization of its finite version, or an accepted prize claim.

Mathematical finite theorem:
  Andras Gyarfas, Graphs with k odd cycle lengths,
  Discrete Mathematics 103 (1992), 41–48.

Existing finite Lean formalization:
  https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs
  commit 8822f7ddef30fadbd92e1c6ab4ed897af356af5e
  src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos58.lean credits Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol.
  The dependency downloader preserves each source's original copyright notice.
  The reproduction script downloads these sources when needed.
  The verified release ZIP also includes all 35 unchanged finite-proof modules.

Graph-coloring compactness:
  This is a standard compactness argument, not a new mathematical result.
  The Lean implementation uses Bhavik Mehta's Rado selection theorem in Mathlib.

Formal Conjectures definitions:
  The included Circumference.lean is byte-identical to the source blob
  08b5da1a82f2e4ad251b18d4c94def8fc715f694 in google-deepmind/formal-conjectures.
  Copyright 2026 The Formal Conjectures Authors; Apache-2.0.
  Its notice is retained verbatim.

Extension and tooling:
  Prepared with OpenAI Codex assistance on 2026-09-17.
  Apache-2.0. No mathematical discovery or first-completion claim is assigned.
  See VERIFICATION.md for the actual build, axiom audit, and replay evidence.

Overlapping earlier arbitrary-graph submission:
  https://github.com/TheJustinSunPrize/awards/pull/130 by MaxwellLaw.
  Its scope overlaps the extension in this package and is explicitly acknowledged.
  Its existence does not establish a committee decision or a prize allocation.

